n we can plot them okay and and we  see the same kind of plot we had before  and we can zoom in to the problem area  and we can see that in green a Chema's  interpolation is nice and smooth and  goes through everything very nicely okay  interpolation also allows you to specify  whether or not your data are periodic  and a Chema's method lets you do that as  well one thing to do that you need to do  in preparing the data to do the  interpolation is in the figure I showed  to interpolate a point number three you  needed two points before and two points  after well that's great in the middle of  your data but at the end points what do  you have out there beyond at each end so  he provides a very nice heuristic to  extrapolate some data points now those  data points that you extrapolate are not  actually used in the interpolation  they're only used to find the  derivatives that you then give to  interpolation okay  so to do a periodic interpolation all I  have to do is wrap the data around  basically take this five and seven and  wrap them out here on the end and take  the three and the two and wrap them over  here to the beginning to get a periodic  interpolation to get enough data to do  that and then just tell it that it's a  periodic interpolation and we can see  giving it just you know a handful you  know five or six points here we can just  use this over a much larger domain and  it's periodic okay what I wanted to use  originally was his spline version and so  I have that one coded up as well again  rewritten from the math and here's an  example of using it get the resource  function and then we'll use it here now  to do a spline it's actually a  parametric plot okay and and he wanted a  way to do the interpolation where you  could have multiple Y values at the same  x-value okay and you can actually have  your line loop all over the place and so  here's an interesting example of that  basically drawing some kind of knot type  of set of data  okay so the motivation for this talk I  wanted to do something outrageous for  the wool from summer school two years  ago and so I thought I could use this  spline on some nice data particular set  of data and get a nice image that we  could do something with so working it  out you know I loaded some data for a  graph and here is the graph okay and  what I did is I found all cycles of size  six and drew a spline around them okay  so instead of having to connect the dots  you know I would have over six points  here a nice smooth curve that would  undulate in and out and stuff like that  and in this case the undulation was what  I was after  okay so we find all the cycles and then  we make the curves and we get what I  call a braided spiky okay the original  points are here in here in here  and there and there and so on you can  see them where they intersect and then  lastly what we wanted to do was the  outrageous thing make t-shirts my  daughter who's who's moved on from  Wolfram was social media manager and she  was there covering the summer school for  that and we would sit down occasionally  at home and work on stuff together she'd  be doing her social media I doing my  consulting work and she would call us  team knock bar and so I had the idea  let's make t-shirts for that okay so I  got to this point and I discovered you  know I realized looking at the code that  we could do more and actually akima had  done more he had done a surface now this  codes not in the repository yet but I do  have it in a package and let me just  quickly show you what it what it can do  we can read in some 2d data and I can  just make a rough list plot of it here  and we can see what it looks like  okay it's just a surface you know some  ridges and valleys and things and we can  interpolate it there's the use its  message usage message for it here's the  interpolation over to D and when we look  at it we can see we have a you know a  nice much smoother plot here's the  contour plot of it then it works very  well the implementation when I rewrote  it is very compact again and if you look  closely at this code and the code from  the 1d you can see that he did X just  the way he did the 1d data then he did Y  by basically doing the same thing but in  the Y direction and then to get the the  cross derivative XY he basically used a  bilinear form on top of the two  one-dimensional derivatives and this is  all in his paper and I said you know we  can probably do this in n dimensions and  so you know that's my postulate I don't  have a proof for it yet but yeah I think  that that we can I've done a lot of work  toward that end and number one I don't  have a proof and hopefully Jonathan and  I will be able to get together this week  and see if we can come up with a proof  that for n n dimensional space if you  take the points of a hypercube in that  space and find all of the first  derivatives to by linear second  derivatives and so on up to D F of W X Y  Z etc in the denominator can you always  fit the cubic polynomial to that now I  demonstrated numerically that I can do  that to any order but I'd love to have a  proof a demonstration is not a proof so  I did a lot of computational exploration  about how could I do this and as I had  said you know in the implementation for  2d we took what we did for the X  direction and then we did the same thing  for y but to do  we use what was called a conjugation I  took what the the Y data I did a  transpose did my operation to get the  derivatives and then transpose back  that's the conjugation step and that way  I can use the same code for X on Y but  but getting things to work the right way  to do this in n dimensional space doing  all that transposition as a lot of extra  work and then the logic gets a little  convoluted it turns out it's much easier  to do if you do it with less convolve  and to do that you know think about edge  detection in images all they're doing is  finding derivatives in the x and y  direction where your image intensity  changes suddenly that's your edge and  we're just doing this in n dimensional  space and so the first thing we need to  do is find the edge detecting kernel for  the problem and here's just an example  of showing what the kernels would look  like for 1 2 3 and 4 dimensions and it's  really just a doing a difference is what  we're going to do and we just have to  nest them the right way to get them to  work at the different dimensions that we  want to use with liske involve and so I  can make some data here for dimensional  data and get the the X values for them  again this is just random okay we get  the size of the space from the length of  dimensions we calculate these kernels  that we will then use we get the  differences in each of the ordinate  directions the deltas as I call them so  we can use those in the denominators we  have to know which level that we're  going to do the convolution at with the  particular kernel and then we just map  the liske involve over each of the  dimensions and then we also use another  map on that result with the deltas to  get the actual  vitae differences or the interval slopes  and if you look at these here the  original dimension was four three five  and seven and each of these is reduced  by one I just want to make one more  point that to figure out how to do some  of the stuff a little bit later on okay  I made a toy problem here where I'm  looking at just symbolically what I'm  going to be doing and to make it a  little bit easier to see I just did it  on symbols of the axes rather than  trying to look at actual you know a 10  dimensional tensor and I can see that  I'm you doing one one first-degree  derivatives than second-degree  derivatives and then third degree in a  fourth degree in fact the third degree  in back to second degree and so on I'm  covering everything but the order is  messy and so with some additional coding  I'm able to extract the different  derivatives of a particular order out so  you get all the first order derivatives  in the order that I know they occur in  all the second and so now I have the  data to give to interpolation in the  right order so I won't go through the  rest of it I'm out of time I will hang  around for questions if you have them I  should you know I can get the 2d  interpolation out on the repository  pretty quickly as it is right now so  it's usable if you want to go that far  I'd love to get the nd dimensional case  worked out and then I'll just have one  interpolation function and you give it  the data it will find a degree and just  do it for you so thank you for your  attention  and I will see you at the reception  thank you  [Applause]  you  [Applause]
 

